>Steve Tiilikainen wrote:

>> All,

>> With books like "The Seven Habits of Highly Effective People" rising to
>> the top of the bestseller lists, I was wondering if the demigod(desse)s
>> among you would be willing to share with the less experienced of us
>> some habits and practices -- good ones that you keep, bad ones that you
>> avoid -- that you believe are, if not essential, extremely beneficial
>> towards the goal of ascension.

>- Here Be Spoilers -

>#1: Don't forget about prayer!

>This is perhaps the most important thing.  Prayer can get you out of an
>absurd number of problems, too many really.

But it comes at a cost. You can't pray too often. You have to not piss
your god off. You can to get in really bad straights to be helped out.

I don't really start praying until I get a coaligned altar. Then frankly
I abuse the altar for all it's worth.

>#2: Keep moving!

>The longer you're in the game without good equipment, the more chance
>the game will send in something nasty you can't handle, or some
>fortuitous combination of other foes.

Not sure about the advise. Do you mean run away?!

>#3: Seek out early wishes!  Magic Lamps can be surprisingly common at
>times, and the minetown lighting store tends to have them frequently.
>ID them by lighting the first lamp you find and seeing if it runs low.
>If it does, name it (Shift-N, then say n to naming only this item)
>"oil".  Since there's only two kinds of lamp in the game, once you've
>ID'd oil, all those that aren't oil must be magic.  Make sure to bless
>them before #rubbing!

You can price ID lamps in a shop. Both oil and magic lamps are ridculously
easy to figure out and usually there's a shop or three high in the dungeon.

Holy water is the tougher find in order to bless. The ways to get holy
water early in the game are dangerous for early characters.

>#4: Know what to wish for!  Unless you can make the dragon scale mail
>you want (out of the appropriate color scales), then your first wish
>should ALWAYS be for either Gray DSM (if you have a cloak of magic
>resistance or some other means of magic resistance -- and aren't
>worried about the Wizard stealing artifact sources of it) or Silver DSM
>(other instances).

There was somewhat of a debate going on in another recent thread. I was
advocating about the same strategy you were outlining above. Another poster
(may have been John) pointed out that reflection is really important in
the early game because nasty ray wands such as sleep, cold, fire, lighting,
and yes even death can appear early in the game. He also pointed out that
unless you're chaotic, there are Artifacts that give good effects and
confer magic resistance. So SDSM may be a better choice in the early game
even though there's a even chance of getting an amulet of reflection
early on in Sokoban.

I tested the theory and I have to agree. I went into wizard mode and
wished up a GDSM along with a wand of death and wand of lightning. I
let a goblin pick up the wands and zap them. While the wand of death
was deflected by the GDSM, the lightning wand turned my character into
crispy critters. However with reflection, both wands vaporized their
zappers, leaving me unharmed.

So unless you actually have reflection, it's the more critical intrinsic
early game. Even as you start to need magic resistance with poly and
level teleport traps, the effects are not too terrible. In fact if you
have SDSM on a poly trap turns you into the dragon.

>  That way, you get a needed intrinsic and a low AC
>as well.  Do not underestimate the worth of a low AC!  You'll be amazed
>how much easier melee seems with DSM.

Agreed there. Low AC is my first short term goal. In ascendable games it
often occurs with the combo of protection in Minetown + an early wish.

>#5: Build a supply of holy water and don't let it run out!  
>#6: Get poison resistance as soon as you can! 
[SNIP]

I certainly agree.

>#7: Do not drink from fountains until you're CERTAINLY you can handle
>the effects!  There's a chance you'll get a wish, but you're much more
>likely to get water moccasins, thieving nymphs or non-friendly water
>demons.  If you play style is to sift through many disposable games
>until you find one with an early wish then this may be acceptable, but
>it isn't for me.  Even mid-level games can be wrecked by these
>monsters, so beware.

Bingo. I always leave fountains alone or generally more effectively turn
them into pools by digging into an out of the beaten path one.

In fact I need to remember to use later game fountains like thrones.

>#8: Always be aware of your get-out-of-jail free cards (GOJFCs).
>Scrolls of teleportation are common and will usually get you out of any
>specific, monster-related peril.  That makes them get out of jail free
>cards, aka "escapes."  A scroll of scare monster is also an escape
>(drop one when a monster is attacking you and note what happens).  A
>one-turn permanent inscribing of Elbereth is also an escape.  Many
>times zapping a wand of digging down, or a wand of teleportation at
>your foe or "." is an escape.  Drinking a potion of full healing is
>like a reset button on the fight.  Always be aware of the number of
>escapes you have left, and always keep at least one out of your
>containers to be used at a moment's notice.  Don't use them
>unnecessarily because you can run out if you abuse them, but it's
>better to use an escape than perish.  (This tip also applies to Rogue.)

This is a hard one to learn. Most times you think that if you can press
one more hit, you can dispatch the threat. That's why I often advocate
learning to play pacifist, not to win, but to learn how to play 
defensively. Early level monsters draw players into the habit of beating
everything you encounter. Learning how to pet dance around with jackals
and foxes makes you very aware that you can leave a tough situation.

>#9: Know which armor and weapons are overrated.
>One of the less-that-perfect things about Nethack is how the game
>throws all kinds of armor at you through the game yet you almost never
>have a reason to wear most of it.  The existence of DSM makes lots of
>armor simply obsolete, since it has no drawbacks while providing the
>best AC in the game.  Hell, mithril is better than most of it, and it's
>more common too since dwarves and elves carry it so often.  In the
>early game, where every point of AC is important and you don't have so
>much stuff that getting weighed down by armor is a problem, then put on
>the best you can find, but once you have mithril drop all the rest.

Unless you're a spellcaster, where mithril makes you virtually useless.

>#10: Know thy enemy!
>Notice the top death reasons at alt.org's list at
>http://alt.org/nethack/topdeaths.html
>Be careful around the monsters at the top of the list until you're
>ready for them.

>Solider Ant: Notorious slayer of low-level characters.  If one appears
>on your level, try to get off the level unless you have good AC.  If
>one gets in melee, use an escape unless you're about level 6 or 7 and
>have a moderately good weapon.

They have speed and a nasty bite.

>Water Moccasin: *Every death* by water moccasin on this list was
>avoidable, simply by not using fountains.  It might be an acceptable
>death if you were using it to make holy water or uncurse something, but
>not if you were just hoping for some generic happy event.

I haven't seen one in so long I've forgotten that the game has them.

>Jackal: These deaths are probably from overwhelmed newbies, but jackals
>can be dangerous to 1st level characters.

Again the "one more hit" mentality. Jackals are pet fodder until the
pet maxes out.

>Wands: The best way to handle these, if the initial blast doesn't kill
>you, is to use an escape.

Bingo. My last wand death was a wand of death in Sokoban trying to get
to reflection...

>Dwarf: Simple, get out of the mines.  The mines can be entertaining to
>explore at very low levels if you still have your pet, but be careful
>not to overexert yourself, flee to the stairs if you need to.

The mines are tough to handle because they have so much open space.
You have to learn to duck and run while you let your buff pet take
care of things in the upper mines. Again a pacifist strategy early
can be helpful.

>Small mimic: The best way to handle these is to be careful in shops and
>build a strong pet.  Still, they're slow enough that often you can
>melee one to death.

While in pre protection mode, I literally lead them out of the shop and
let them re-mimic outside the shop. You almost never have to melee them
unless you just want to.

